Description: Quick: draw a window whose elements show depth once per eye
 .
 Stereo KWin shows depth inside a surface when a toolkit draws the surface's
 elements once per eye and hands both views over as the desktop's own depth;
 the edition's Qt Widgets does this for raster windows. A QQuickWindow drew
 one picture, into the left buffer only.
 .
 A style that shows the depth of a window's elements sets the window's
 _q_stereo_depth while it does, and gives each item that lies off the screen's
 plane its parallax, _q_stereo_parallax, in device pixels, positive when
 nearer. The window then asks the platform for two views of its surface
 (_q_stereo_views), and once the surface has them it is drawn as a pair
 (_q_stereo_pair): each eye is polished, synchronized and rendered in a pass
 of its own, with the window's _q_stereo_eye telling the style which. The
 left eye ends a frame of its own that is not presented, since what the right
 eye's synchronization updates is read when a frame's commands are submitted;
 the right eye is rendered into the right buffer and presents both.
 .
 For each eye an item with a parallax moves sideways by its share of the
 parallax relative to what it lies on, its nearest ancestor with a parallax or
 else the window, split as Stereo KWin splits a window's parallax: the right
 eye by whole pixels, the left eye the rest. An item deeper than what it lies
 on is cut to its own rectangle where it is in 2D, the opening it is seen
 through, an item that draws itself too. Only the nodes move: the items'
 geometry, input and mapping stay as in 2D.
 .
 Both render loops draw the pair; in the threaded one the gui thread polishes
 and synchronizes the right eye while the render thread renders the left, and
 a repaint asked for on the render thread becomes a whole frame. What setting
 an eye dirties is drawn by the frame that sets it and asks for no frame of
 its own, so a still window stays still. Until the surface has its two views,
 and in a frame drawn for an expose, both eyes show the window's one picture.
 A window whose style shows no depth, or whose platform gives its surface one
 view, is drawn exactly as before. The state is kept beside the window's and
 the items' own, which keeps their layouts.
